---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import CreditCardPayoffCalculator from '@components/islands/CreditCardPayoffCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('finance', 'credit-card-payoff-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How long will it take to pay off my credit card?', answer: 'Enter your balance, APR and monthly payment under "Monthly payment", and the calculator shows the payoff time and the total interest you will pay. Switch to "Target payoff time" to find the payment needed to clear it by a target date.' },
  { question: 'What happens if I only pay the minimum?', answer: 'Minimum payments are designed to keep you in debt as long as possible. On a high balance, paying only the minimum can take over a decade and cost more in interest than the original purchase. Paying even a little extra each month dramatically shortens the timeline.' },
  { question: 'How can I pay off credit card debt faster?', answer: 'Pay more than the minimum, and if you have several cards use a payoff order: the avalanche method (highest APR first) costs the least interest, while the snowball method (smallest balance first) gives quicker wins for motivation. A 0% balance-transfer card can also pause interest while you clear the principal.' },
  { question: 'Should I use a balance transfer?', answer: 'A 0% introductory-APR balance transfer can save a lot of interest if you clear the balance before the promotional period ends. Weigh the transfer fee (typically 3–5%) and be sure the payment you can afford will actually pay it off in time — the rate jumps once the intro period is over.' },
  { question: 'Will credit card companies negotiate a payoff?', answer: 'Sometimes — but usually only on seriously delinquent accounts, where a lender may accept a lump-sum settlement for less than the full balance. It is not an option for accounts in good standing, and a settlement can hurt your credit score, so treat it as a last resort rather than a strategy.' },
  { question: 'How is credit card interest calculated?', answer: 'Card interest is quoted as an APR but usually charged daily on your balance. Divide the APR by 365 for the daily rate, and the issuer applies it to your average balance each day, so carrying a balance even a few days costs interest. Paying the statement in full each month avoids it entirely.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  intro="See how long it takes to pay off a credit card and what it costs in interest — or the monthly payment needed to be debt-free by a set date."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-07-22' }}
>
  <CreditCardPayoffCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Under <strong>Calculate by</strong>, choose <strong>Monthly payment</strong> or <strong>Target payoff time</strong>.</li>
      <li>Enter your <strong>card balance</strong> and its <strong>APR</strong>.</li>
      <li>In Monthly-payment mode, add the <strong>payment</strong> you will make each month; in Target mode, add the number of <strong>months</strong> you want to be debt-free in.</li>
      <li>Read the payoff time (or the required payment) along with the total interest you'll pay.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Either way, the goal is the same: pay as far above the monthly interest charge as you can, so
      more of every payment attacks the balance.
    </p>

    <h2>How credit card interest works</h2>
    <p>
      Card interest is quoted as an <strong>APR</strong> but usually charged on your balance, so
      carrying a balance costs money every month. Divide the APR by 12 for the monthly rate: a 22%
      APR is about 1.83% a month. Because the charge is based on what you owe, the interest shrinks
      as the balance falls — which is why extra payments snowball. Paying the statement in full each
      month avoids interest entirely.
    </p>

    <h2>The formula</h2>
    <p>In Monthly-payment mode, the months to clear the balance are:</p>
    <p><strong>n = −ln(1 − B · r / M) / ln(1 + r)</strong></p>
    <p>In Target mode, the required payment uses the amortization formula:</p>
    <p><strong>M = B · r · (1 + r)<sup>n</sup> / ((1 + r)<sup>n</sup> − 1)</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>B</strong> — the current card balance</li>
      <li><strong>r</strong> — the monthly rate (APR ÷ 12)</li>
      <li><strong>M</strong> — the monthly payment</li>
      <li><strong>n</strong> — the number of months</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>A worked example: the minimum-payment trap</h2>
    <p>
      Take a <strong>$5,000</strong> balance at a typical <strong>22% APR</strong>. The first
      month's interest alone is $5,000 × (22% ÷ 12) ≈ <strong>$91.67</strong>. Pay
      <strong>$120</strong> and only about <strong>$28</strong> reduces the balance — the payoff
      drags out to about <strong>6 years, 8 months</strong> and roughly <strong>$4,600</strong> in
      interest, nearly the size of the original balance.
    </p>
    <p>
      Bump the payment to <strong>$250</strong> and about $158 of the first payment hits principal.
      The same debt clears in roughly <strong>2 years, 2 months</strong> for about
      <strong>$1,500</strong> of interest. That is the entire game: every dollar above the interest
      charge attacks the principal directly.
    </p>

    <h2>Setting a target payoff date</h2>
    <p>
      Switch to <strong>Target payoff time</strong> to work backwards. To clear that same
      <strong>$5,000</strong> at <strong>22%</strong> in <strong>24 months</strong>, the calculator
      returns a required payment of about <strong>$259.39 a month</strong>, for roughly
      <strong>$1,225</strong> of total interest. Shorten the target and the payment rises but the
      interest falls — a direct way to price the trade-off between monthly cash and total cost.
    </p>

    <h2>Strategies to clear it faster</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Avalanche</strong> — with multiple cards, pay extra on the highest-APR card first. This costs the least total interest.</li>
      <li><strong>Snowball</strong> — pay the smallest balance first for a quick, motivating win, then roll that payment into the next card.</li>
      <li><strong>Balance transfer</strong> — a 0% intro-APR card pauses interest; clear the balance before the promo ends and mind the transfer fee (typically 3–5%).</li>
      <li><strong>Pay biweekly</strong> — half-payments every two weeks make one extra monthly payment a year and cut the balance the interest is charged on.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why paying it down beats saving</h2>
    <p>
      If you carry a balance at 20% or more, clearing it is effectively a guaranteed, tax-free 20%
      return — more than almost any investment reliably earns. Prioritising high-interest debt is
      usually the highest-value thing you can do with a spare dollar. To see that same compounding
      working <em>for</em> you instead, use the
      <a href="/finance/compound-interest-calculator">compound interest calculator</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It assumes a <strong>fixed payment</strong> and a <strong>fixed APR</strong>; real minimum payments shrink as the balance falls, which stretches payoff much longer.</li>
      <li>It assumes you <strong>stop adding new charges</strong> — fresh purchases restart the cycle.</li>
      <li>Payoff totals assume a <strong>full final payment</strong>, so they may slightly overstate the last payment and the interest.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      For a general fixed-rate debt with a set term, see the
      <a href="/finance/payment-calculator">payment calculator</a>; to grow savings once the cards
      are clear, try the <a href="/finance/savings-calculator">savings calculator</a>. This is
      general educational information, not financial advice.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
